language ESSENCE' 1.0
$ resource_constrained_project_scheduling: an Essence' model from DCP Rosetta.
$
$ Schedule a set of jobs, each with a duration and a resource requirement per resource type, so that the total
$ project duration (makespan) is as small as possible. A job can start only after each of its predecessors has finished,
$ and at every time the jobs running use no more of each resource than its capacity. Find the start time of each job.
$
$ Save this file as model.eprime. Download `resource_constrained_project_scheduling.json` and `dcp_convert_essence.py` from the
$ problem's page:
$   https://dcp-bench.github.io/DCP-Rosetta/problems/resource_constrained_project_scheduling.html
$ Convert the instances once, then run the first one with Savile Row 1.11 (it needs Java); the
$ solution is written to `resource_constrained_project_scheduling_1.param.solution`:
$   python dcp_convert_essence.py resource_constrained_project_scheduling.json
$   savilerow model.eprime resource_constrained_project_scheduling_1.param -run-solver

$ durations_data_dim1: the number of jobs J (derived from the data); the data numbers jobs from 0, here job j is index j
given durations_data_dim1 : int
$ durations_data[j]: the duration of job j (indices from 1)
given durations_data : matrix indexed by [int(1..durations_data_dim1)] of int(0..)
$ resource_capacities_data_dim1: the number of resource types R (derived from the data)
given resource_capacities_data_dim1 : int
$ resource_capacities_data[r]: the capacity of resource r (indices from 1)
given resource_capacities_data : matrix indexed by [int(1..resource_capacities_data_dim1)] of int(0..)
$ resource_needs_data[j,r]: the units of resource r that job j uses while it runs (indices from 1)
given resource_needs_data : matrix indexed by [int(1..durations_data_dim1), int(1..resource_capacities_data_dim1)] of int(0..)
$ successors_link_data_dim1: the number of precedence pairs E (derived from the data)
given successors_link_data_dim1 : int
$ successors_link_data[e,1] is a job that must finish before successors_link_data[e,2] starts; the data numbers jobs from 0
given successors_link_data : matrix indexed by [int(1..successors_link_data_dim1), int(1..2)] of int(0..)

$ start_time[j]: the start time of job j, counted from 0 (indices from 1)
find start_time : matrix indexed by [int(1..durations_data_dim1)] of int(0..sum(durations_data))
$ makespan: the finish time of the last job
find makespan : int(0..sum(durations_data))

minimising makespan
such that
$ each precedence pair: the first job finishes before the second one starts (the data's job numbers are shifted by 1)
forAll e : int(1..successors_link_data_dim1) . start_time[successors_link_data[e,1]+1] + durations_data[successors_link_data[e,1]+1] <= start_time[successors_link_data[e,2]+1],
$ every job finishes by the makespan
forAll j : int(1..durations_data_dim1) . start_time[j] + durations_data[j] <= makespan,
$ at every time the jobs running on each resource use no more than its capacity
forAll r : int(1..resource_capacities_data_dim1) . cumulative(start_time, durations_data, [resource_needs_data[j,r] | j : int(1..durations_data_dim1)], resource_capacities_data[r])
